"# Exercise 4: Parallel Arrays & Custom Song Sequencer\\n",
"\\n",
"Instead of triggering every note manually, electronic music relies on **sequencers**—automated loops that step through predefined lists of pitches and durations.\\n",
"\\n",
"## Exploring `SYNTH_SEQUENCER.ino`\\n",
"\\n",
"1. Unzip `SYNTH_SEQUENCER.zip` and open `SYNTH_SEQUENCER.ino` in the Arduino IDE.\\n",
"2. Select **Seeeduino Nano** and your port, then upload the sketch.\\n",
"3. Press the buttons on your PCB to trigger the built-in preset melodies. Turn the **ADSR** and **Resonance** dials while a melody plays to hear how articulation changes the character of the song.\\n",
"\\n",
"### How Parallel Arrays Store Music\\n",
"\\n",
"In `SYNTH_SEQUENCER.ino`, each melody is stored across two **parallel arrays** of identical length:\\n",
"\* `NOTES_X[]`: Stores the pitch constants (e.g., `NOTE_C4`, `NOTE_E4`, `REST`).\\n",
"\* `TIMES_X[]`: Stores the corresponding duration for each note.\\n",
"\\n"
\]
